          "derivation": "The two forms are real Book Pahlavi words and both begin with ham- < *sem-, but they are not synonyms and the gloss fits only one of them. hamahl [h(m)hl] is, per MacKenzie, simply a variant spelling of hamāl 'equal, peer; comrade' — so 'equal, similar' is right for it. hamēmāl [hmymʾl], on the other hand, is a legal term meaning 'opponent, adversary', with the abstract hamēmālīh 'opposition'; it is the counterpart of pēšēmāl 'plaintiff' and pasēmāl 'defendant, accused' (MacKenzie derives both explicitly 'v. hamēmāl'), and Nyberg glosses it 'adversary'. The compound is ham- 'same, co-' + -ēmāl, i.e. the party who has the same (matching) claim in a suit — the semantic step 'matched with' > 'opposing party' is the same one as in Avestan ham-ərəθa- 'adversary' and Old Persian hamara- 'enemy' from the same preverb. So Nourai is right that hamēmāl belongs to *sem-, and right that hamahl means 'equal', but wrong to gloss hamēmāl 'equal, similar'. On hamahl/hamāl itself, Nyberg reads the word *ham-hāl and would derive it from *hama-harza- (i.e. from the *selg- root of entry 0 on this same page), 'Another reading and etymology by Bthl' — a minority view; MacKenzie's hamahl = hamāl is the standard one.",
